hts into  things we never would have expected webb  will replace this the hubble telescope  the new space telescope is a hundred  times more powerful with a mirror that  must unfold outside the rocket it's  outfitted with a solar shield to block  heat and light but will be so far beyond  the moon that it can't be fixed if  anything goes wrong we're not going to  have the second chance  like we did with hubble so it has to  work on the first go and that's what  makes the next month of space flight  nerve-wracking for scientists all over  the world i'm really just  on the edge of my seat the nervousness  starts with the expected liftoff  tomorrow morning eric sorensen global  news toronto  what if all three of your vaccine doses  are different brands still ahead the  experts answer your covid questions  bc's provincial health officer had a  message today for those considering  whether to ignore public health advice  and attend large gatherings with friends  and family over the holidays dr bonnie  henry says there's still so much we  don't know about omicron  omochromis is different it is spreading  in a way that is very different from  previous variants in a sense we're in a  different game a different pandemic now  and the one big question that we don't  have yet and fully understand yet is how  severe this is going to get  what's the impact going to be on our  hospitals and speaking of questions all  week viewers have been sending in their  questions to us about the new variant  and to help answer some of your covid  christmas questions we are joined now by  epidemiologist dr christopher labos who  joins me now from montreal dr labos  thanks so much for joining us let's get  right to the first question and a timely  one for those planning to sit down for  some christmas dinner this evening this  one comes from jack who asks can you get  infected if someone who is infected were  to breathe or cough or handle your food  so from what we know no  it's not transmitted in food you can't  contaminate someone's food the issue  with family gatherings and gathering  around a meal is that you're sitting  around the table with people who are all  breathing the same air as you so that's  the real danger it's not really a  question of contact meaning you're  touching surfaces or you're eating  contaminated food or anything of that  kind  okay good to know and another question  this one from joanne of course a lot of  people wondering how they can socialize  safely over the holidays she wants to  know are you okay not wearing a mask if  you're outside talking with people for  say five to ten minutes and you're not  social distancing  yeah that's probably okay i mean here's  the thing we have to remember enough  it's never a question of safe or  dangerous it's always a question of  degrees of risk so if you're outside the  risk is significantly decreased that  being said it's still  there is still some value in keeping a  bit of a distance from people if you're  outside in a large crowd surrounded by  people and you're in close contact with  them that's probably not ideal but if  you can keep at least a respectful  distance and you're outside the risk of  one person passing covert onto another  is probably pretty low especially if you  keep that interaction short like only a  few minutes long  okay uh next question comes from jan  we've received a lot of questions like  this one uh people concerned about  mixing doses especially as they go to  get their booster shot so for jan she's  in nova scotia  where there are more moderna shots and  her first shot was astrazeneca second  was pfizer are there any considerations  or implications in mixing thrice over  uh the short answer is uh no there  doesn't the we look pfizer and modernist  seem to be interchangeable and there's  no real reason to prefer one over the  other and there's no harm if you've got  you know an astrazeneca followed by  something else so i wouldn't worry about  this in any way shape or form  right this next question comes from  karen and obviously one that a lot of  people are wondering is that people try  and get their hands on those precious  rapid tests but karen's been hearing  more and more about the high rate of  false negatives that we see with the  rapid tests so why is it that these  tests are still being promoted as a way  to know whether it's safe to gather  so the rapid tests are not perfect there  can be false negatives and there can be  false positives so they're not perfect  but they are still useful so we  shouldn't overstate their benefit but we  shouldn't understate it either it is a  useful test if you use it in conjunction  with everything else so if you have if  you are fully vaccinated you've gotten  your booster you're wearing a mask  you're keeping gathering small you're in  a well-ventilated area and everybody at  that around that table tests negative  that's one extra layer of protection so  remember it's not perfect but a test  doesn't have to be perfect in order to  be useful  yeah i remember seeing one analysis  that's that found that i think was about  60 percent effectiveness for those rapid  antigen tests for people without  symptoms so as you say an imperfect tool  but an important one nonetheless last  question for you comes from ken who says  that at the beginning of the pandemic  there was some talk about achieving herd  immunity and ken wants to know will we  ever reach and achieve herd immunity  well it sort of depends it depends on  the virus it depends on the new variance  it depends what the ultimate level of  vaccination rate we achieve we still  have a ways to go before we vaccinate  the entire population so we're really  not quite there yet and you know the  point of hurt immunity is not that the  virus is going to disappear the point of  hurt immunity is that we have enough  vaccinated people to protect a small  number of people who can't be vaccinated  so i think the short answer is that  question is we will wait and see i don't  think anybody really knows how this  pandemic is going to play out over the  next six to 12 months  yeah one thing we've learned to expect  is the unexpected that's for sure over  the past couple of years dr christopher  lavos in montreal thank you so much for  taking the time to chat with us  especially on christmas eve really  appreciate it merry christmas  merry christmas to you too  next a feast for the eyes hidden in  plain sight  [Music]  well take a look at this a drone  captured this bird's eye view of festive  fort st john bc transformed into a  winter wonderland to support the  salvation army  it really is a beautiful time of year  though in the cold of winter you can be  forgiven for keeping your head down but  one ottawa artist is trying to change  that she calls herself street art  miniature or sam for short and as  michael couture explains sam is hoping  people will take a moment to take in the  beauty of their surroundings  it's been said that art is all around us  even if we hardly notice while hunched  over our phones  the size of some murals or sculptures  make them hard to miss but if you look  hard enough around ottawa you'll find  some masterpieces are many  these little clay creations are hidden  around ottawa in plain sight however  they're hard to spot because most are  the size of a dime or fingernail and  making them is no easy task a lollipop  can take a couple of minutes something  like a charcuterie board though could  take up to three to four hours depending  on the different pieces she goes by sam  or street art miniature the civil  servant works surreptitiously so we've  agreed to keep her identity secret  often she'll place a piece at night or  quickly during the day and like other  street artists she wants her work to be  the focus i want to stop someone in  their day and think what why is that  there a tiny hot dog on a brick wall  that's strange the juxtaposition of it  is just so odd  she does give clues about where she's  left the clay carvings and the minis  have a major following john normando has  hunted them with his partner since 2017  and they head out every time there's a  new post  you want to go see it in person  just to  appreciate it in person but also we  found that we really started  appreciating the actual hunt itself as  well normando isn't her only fan the  makers of a locally made milk-based  vodka collaborated with sam for a major  mini marketing campaign obviously we're  not going to be able to compete with the  big guys in terms of spending so we have  to do something that's a little  different and a little interesting and  you know if it could also mean  supporting a local artist and  doing something fun then all the better  and that's sam's goal to bring a little  fun to people's day especially during  the pandemic trying to encourage people  to go out and explore their  neighborhoods and just go for a walk get  some fresh air  try to enjoy yourself during this time  and to keep an eye on your surroundings  because you never know what you may find  mike lacrouter global news ottawa  making me hungry that is global national  for this christmas eve i'm jeff semple  we know it's been a difficult time so we  wanted to leave you tonight with a  little levity we asked you our global  news community to capture your furry  friends wearing their festive best and  you delivered cats dogs even budgies  showing off their christmas spirit so  thank you for sharing those and thank  you for watching colleen christy will be  at the anchor desk here tomorrow have a  safe and merry christmas and a happy new  year  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

